---
import DocsLayout from "../../components/DocsLayout.astro";
import CodeBlock from "../../components/CodeBlock.astro";

const seam = `import type { MdbaseClient } from "@mdbase-dev/sdk";

// Accept the client, not a connector, so the same code runs against
// a real collection and against MemoryReplica in tests.
export async function listOpenTasks(db: MdbaseClient) {
  const page = await db.query({
    types: ["task"],
    where: 'status == "open"',
    order_by: [{ field: "due", direction: "asc" }],
    limit: 50,
  });
  return page.records;
}`;
---

<DocsLayout
  title="SDK overview"
  description="Build apps whose data lives in the user’s own mdbase collection. @mdbase-dev/sdk is the TypeScript client: local reads, optimistic writes, live queries and sign-in."
  active="overview"
  headings={[
    { href: "#why", label: "Why build on mdbase" },
    { href: "#start", label: "Where to start" },
    { href: "#model", label: "How the SDK works" },
    { href: "#choose-a-surface", label: "Getting a client" },
    { href: "#application-shape", label: "Application shape" },
    { href: "#package-map", label: "Packages" },
    { href: "#publish", label: "Publishing an app" },
    { href: "#status", label: "Stability" },
    { href: "#help", label: "Getting help" }
  ]}
>
  <p>
    An mdbase collection is a folder of Markdown files with typed
    frontmatter, synced across a person’s devices. An app built with the SDK
    reads and writes the records in a collection its user chooses.
  </p>

  <h2 id="why">Why build on mdbase</h2>
  <ul>
    <li>
      <strong>You run no backend.</strong> The user’s collection is your
      app’s database. A finished app is a folder of static files: no server,
      no database to operate and no API key to guard.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Sign-in, sync and offline are done.</strong> The SDK signs the
      user in, keeps a copy of the collection on the device, saves writes
      there at once and syncs them when it can. Edits made on two devices
      merge field by field, and a value that loses is kept for review.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Users bring their data, and keep it.</strong> Records are
      Markdown files the user owns. They can open them in mdbase’s
      {" "}<a href="/apps/">Editor, Reader and Writer</a>, in Obsidian, or in
      a text editor, and they still have them if they stop using your app.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Apps can share records.</strong> A
      {" "}<a href="/sdk/contracts/">contract</a> names a shape of record,
      such as a task. Two apps that use the same contract work on the same
      records in one collection, with no integration between them.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Access is the user’s decision.</strong> Your app asks for the
      capabilities in its <a href="/sdk/manifest/">manifest</a>, the user
      approves them for one collection, and can withdraw them later. See the
      {" "}<a href="/sdk/security/">security model</a>.
    </li>
  </ul>

  <h2 id="start">Where to start</h2>
  <div class="start-options">
    <div>
      <strong>Quickstart</strong>
      <p>
        Run a short script against an in-memory collection. It needs no
        account or service, and shows reads, writes and live queries.
      </p>
      <a href="/sdk/quickstart/">Run the quickstart</a>
    </div>
    <div>
      <strong>Build an app</strong>
      <p>
        Build Worklog, a small React app, from an empty folder to a deployed
        site: types, manifest, sign-in, live list and writes.
      </p>
      <a href="/sdk/connect-quickstart/">Follow the tutorial</a>
    </div>
    <div>
      <strong>Develop on localhost</strong>
      <p>
        Run your app from a development server against real accounts and
        collections.
      </p>
      <a href="/sdk/local-development/">Set up local development</a>
    </div>
    <div>
      <strong>The format</strong>
      <p>
        Build tools that read and write mdbase collections as files, with no
        SDK.
      </p>
      <a href="/spec/">Read the specification</a>
    </div>
  </div>

  <h2 id="model">How the SDK works</h2>
  <p>
    Your app talks to a <strong>replica</strong> of the collection: a copy
    that applies changes in the order the collection’s log confirms them. The
    SDK gives you that replica’s view, plus your own writes that are still
    waiting.
  </p>
  <ul>
    <li>
      <strong>Reads are local.</strong> <code>get</code>, <code>query</code>
      {" "}and <code>live</code> answer from the replica’s view. They include
      your pending writes.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Writes are intents.</strong> Each write returns at once with a
      receipt in the <code>pending</code> state. It becomes
      {" "}<code>confirmed</code> when the log accepts it, or
      {" "}<code>rejected</code> with a reason.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Nothing is lost silently.</strong> Concurrent edits to the same
      field are merged, and the losing value is kept as a conflict you can
      review. A file-backed replica can also put a local edit on hold instead
      of overwriting it.
    </li>
  </ul>
  <p>
    <a href="/sdk/offline-sync/">Pending writes and sync status</a> covers
    receipts, holds and conflicts.
  </p>

  <h2 id="choose-a-surface">Getting a client</h2>
  <p>
    Every client comes from <code>connect()</code>, given a
    {" "}<strong>connector</strong> that says how to reach a replica:
  </p>
  <table class="api-table">
    <thead>
      <tr>
        <th>You are building</th>
        <th>How you reach the collection</th>
      </tr>
    </thead>
    <tbody>
      <tr>
        <td>A web or mobile app</td>
        <td>
          <code>createMdbase()</code> signs the user in and hands you a
          connection. Its client is an ordinary <code>MdbaseClient</code>.
          See <a href="/sdk/connect-quickstart/">Build an app</a>.
        </td>
      </tr>
      <tr>
        <td>A script or plugin on a computer running the mdbase desktop app</td>
        <td>
          Connectors from <code>@mdbase-dev/sdk/node</code> reach the desktop
          app’s replica on the same machine. See
          {" "}<a href="/sdk/routing/">How apps reach a collection</a>.
        </td>
      </tr>
      <tr>
        <td>Tests and demo modes</td>
        <td>
          <code>MemoryReplica</code> from <code>@mdbase-dev/sdk/testing</code>.
          See <a href="/sdk/testing/">Testing</a>.
        </td>
      </tr>
    </tbody>
  </table>

  <h2 id="application-shape">Application shape</h2>
  <p>
    Keep domain code on <code>MdbaseClient</code>. It then runs unchanged
    against any connector.
  </p>
  <CodeBlock code={seam} language="ts" label="tasks.ts" />

  <h2 id="package-map">Packages</h2>
  <table class="api-table">
    <thead>
      <tr>
        <th>Package</th>
        <th>What it is for</th>
      </tr>
    </thead>
    <tbody>
      <tr>
        <td><code>@mdbase-dev/sdk</code></td>
        <td>
          The client. <code>createMdbase</code> for apps,
          {" "}<code>connect</code> and <code>MdbaseClient</code> for
          everything that reads and writes records.
          {" "}<a href="/sdk/api/">API reference</a>.
        </td>
      </tr>
      <tr>
        <td><code>@mdbase-dev/ui</code></td>
        <td>
          The sign-in, collection picker, approval and setup screens as React
          components. <a href="/sdk/ui/">UI components</a>.
        </td>
      </tr>
      <tr>
        <td><code>@mdbase-dev/connect-dev</code></td>
        <td>
          Build-time tools: the manifest checker and the type pack builder.
          {" "}<a href="/sdk/connect-dev/">Developer kit</a>.
        </td>
      </tr>
    </tbody>
  </table>
  <p>The SDK has several entry points:</p>
  <table class="api-table">
    <thead>
      <tr>
        <th>Import</th>
        <th>What it gives you</th>
      </tr>
    </thead>
    <tbody>
      <tr>
        <td><code>@mdbase-dev/sdk</code></td>
        <td>
          <code>createMdbase</code>, <code>connect</code>,
          {" "}<code>MdbaseClient</code>, errors and status helpers
        </td>
      </tr>
      <tr>
        <td><code>@mdbase-dev/sdk/describe</code></td>
        <td><code>describeError</code>: a title, message and action for any failure</td>
      </tr>
      <tr>
        <td><code>@mdbase-dev/sdk/testing</code></td>
        <td><code>MemoryReplica</code> and test fixtures</td>
      </tr>
      <tr>
        <td><code>@mdbase-dev/sdk/node</code></td>
        <td>Connectors to the desktop app over local IPC or localhost</td>
      </tr>
      <tr>
        <td><code>@mdbase-dev/sdk/browser</code></td>
        <td>
          The same surface as a browser module. The package also ships a
          classic-script bundle; see <a href="/sdk/portable-apps/">Browser bundle</a>.
        </td>
      </tr>
      <tr>
        <td><code>@mdbase-dev/sdk/account</code></td>
        <td>Encryption password and recovery key set-up for Private collections</td>
      </tr>
    </tbody>
  </table>
  <p>
    The SDK needs Node 20 or later outside the browser. It is what apps use
    instead of the <code>@mdbase-dev/connect</code> beta.
  </p>

  <h2 id="publish">Publishing an app</h2>
  <p>
    There is no review, registration form or API key. The SDK registers your
    manifest with mdbase the first time your app starts, and each user
    approves the app for their own collection. To publish, deploy the built
    files to an HTTPS origin; the
    {" "}<a href="/sdk/connect-quickstart/#deploy">tutorial</a> ends with
    that step.
  </p>
  <p>
    The approval screen shows your app’s name and the site it is served
    from. mdbase does not verify who publishes an app, and an application ID
    is a label, so serve your app from an origin your users recognise.
  </p>
  <p>
    The <a href="/apps/">apps page</a> lists the apps mdbase makes. There is
    no directory of other apps yet. If you build one, write to
    {" "}<a href="mailto:callum@mdbase.dev">callum@mdbase.dev</a>.
  </p>

  <h2 id="status">Stability</h2>
  <p>
    <code>@mdbase-dev/sdk</code>, <code>@mdbase-dev/ui</code> and
    {" "}<code>@mdbase-dev/connect-dev</code> are in beta on npm.
  </p>
  <ul>
    <li>
      The API can change between betas. Pin exact versions in an app you
      ship, and read the
      {" "}<a href="https://github.com/mdbase-dev/mdbase-connect/releases">release notes</a>
      {" "}before you upgrade.
    </li>
    <li>
      Publishing a new beta does not change an app that is already
      deployed; it keeps the SDK it was built with. If the service ever needs
      a newer one, the SDK reports a problem whose action is
      {" "}<code>update_app</code>.
    </li>
    <li>
      The <a href="/spec/">collection format</a> is specified separately and
      does not change with SDK releases. Records your app writes stay
      readable.
    </li>
  </ul>

  <h2 id="help">Getting help</h2>
  <ul>
    <li>
      Report a bug or ask a question in
      {" "}<a href="https://github.com/mdbase-dev/mdbase-connect/issues">GitHub issues</a>.
    </li>
    <li>
      Email <a href="mailto:callum@mdbase.dev">callum@mdbase.dev</a>.
    </li>
    <li>
      Report a security problem as the
      {" "}<a href="/security/">security page</a> describes.
    </li>
  </ul>
</DocsLayout>
